static const char text_keys [] =
	"id: keys\n"
	"title: Key bindings\n"
	"summary: The actions of the editor, their default keys and how to chan"
	"ge them.\n"
	"keywords: keys, bindings, map, unmap, shortcut, rebind, keyboard, sett"
	"ings, mlcfg\n"
	"see: editor, commands\n"
	"\n"
	"Every control key of the editor is bound to a named action. The table "
	"below\n"
	"lists the actions with the keys they have when nothing is changed;\n"
	"`:keys` shows the table as it is now.\n"
	"\n"
	"```keys\n"
	"undo         Ctrl+Z                         undo the last change\n"
	"redo         Ctrl+Shift+Z, Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+R   redo what was undone\n"
	"block-new    Ctrl+Enter, Shift+Enter        start a block, last domain\n"
	"block-pick   Ctrl+B                         start a block, pick the do"
	"main\n"
	"note-toggle  Ctrl+N                         make the line a note, or b"
	"ack\n"
	"domain-next  Alt+Right                      the next domain of the lin"
	"e\n"
	"domain-prev  Alt+Left                       the previous domain of the"
	" line\n"
	"lines-down   Alt+Down                       move the selected lines do"
	"wn\n"
	"lines-up     Alt+Up                         move the selected lines up\n"
	"fold         Alt+Enter                      fold or unfold the block\n"
	"block-next   Ctrl+Down                      go to the next block\n"
	"block-prev   Ctrl+Up                        go to the previous block\n"
	"goto-first   Ctrl+Home                      go to the first line\n"
	"goto-last    Ctrl+End                       go to the last line\n"
	"select-line  Ctrl+L                         select the line\n"
	"select-all   Ctrl+A                         select everything\n"
	"copy         Ctrl+C                         copy the selection or the "
	"line\n"
	"cut          Ctrl+X                         cut the selection or the l"
	"ine\n"
	"paste        Ctrl+V                         paste\n"
	"command      Ctrl+P                         open the command line\n"
	"save         Ctrl+S                         save the session\n"
	"quit         Ctrl+Q                         save and quit\n"
	"steps        F3                             show or hide the line's st"
	"eps\n"
	"help         F1                             open the help\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Changing a key\n"
	"`:map KEY ACTION` binds a key, `:unmap KEY` removes the binding of a k"
	"ey.\n"
	"Both are written to the `[keys]` section of the settings file, one lin"
	"e per\n"
	"key, and are read again at the next start:\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"[keys]\n"
	"Ctrl+K = command\n"
	"Ctrl+Z = none\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"`none` removes a key, so the example gives Ctrl+K the command line and\n"
	"leaves Ctrl+Z without an action. A key bound twice keeps the later lin"
	"e.\n"
	"A line that is not a key or not an action is ignored, and the first on"
	"e is\n"
	"shown in the status line when the program starts.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Writing a key\n"
	"Modifiers joined with `+`, then the key: `Ctrl+Shift+Z`, `Alt+Left`,\n"
	"`F3`, `Ctrl+Enter`. Letters are not case sensitive. The names of the o"
	"ther\n"
	"keys are Enter, Esc, Tab, Backspace, Delete, Insert, Left, Right, Up, "
	"Down,\n"
	"Home, End, PageUp, PageDown and F1 to F12.\n"
	"\n"
	"Only keys that cannot be typed as text can be bound: a key needs Ctrl "
	"or\n"
	"Alt, or is a function key, or is Shift+Enter. Plain characters, the\n"
	"arrows, Enter, Esc, Backspace and Shift with a movement key stay as th"
	"ey\n"
	"are.\n";

static const char text_lambda [] =
	"id: lambda\n"
	"title: Lambda calculus\n"
	"summary: Terms, abstraction, application and step by step reduction.\n"
	"keywords: lambda, calculus, beta, reduction, church, numeral, normal f"
	"orm, trace\n"
	"see: boolean, syntax\n"
	"\n"
	"A line in a block of the lambda domain is a term. The result is its no"
	"rmal\n"
	"form, found by normal order reduction.\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"x            a name\n"
	"\316\273x. t        an abstraction (also \\x. t); \316\273x y. t is "
	"\316\273x. \316\273y. t\n"
	"t u          an application: f x y is (f x) y\n"
	"3            the Church numeral \316\273f x. f (f (f x))\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"The body of a \316\273 goes as far to the right as it can, and an appl"
	"ication\n"
	"binds tighter than a \316\273. The result is written with as few paren"
	"theses as\n"
	"possible, and a binder gets a prime (`y'`) when its name would capture"
	" a\n"
	"free one:\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"(\316\273x y. x) y\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"gives `\316\273y'. y`.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Definitions\n"
	"`name = term` defines a name for the lines below. A \316\273 that bind"
	"s the same\n"
	"name hides the definition. A definition is kept as the term, not as it"
	"s\n"
	"normal form, so a term without one can be named:\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"succ = \316\273n f x. f (n f x)\n"
	"plus = \316\273m n f x. m f (n f x)\n"
	"plus 2 3\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"The last line gives `\316\273f x. f (f (f (f (f x))))`, the Church num"
	"eral 5.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Steps\n"
	"`trace` before a term shows every beta step under the line, up to 60.\n"
	"You do not need to write it: `F3` on a line of the lambda domain shows"
	" the\n"
	"steps of its reduction, and `matlog steps --domain lambda TERM` prints\n"
	"them.\n"
	"\n"
	"# While you type\n"
	"The normal form appears dimmed after the term as soon as it is complet"
	"e.\n"
	"The `\316\273` and the names it binds have the colour of the class `bi"
	"nder`.\n"
	"\n"
	"# When there is no normal form\n"
	"After 5000 steps the line is the error `lambda_no_normal_form`, and wh"
	"en\n"
	"the term grows past two million nodes `lambda_too_big`. A definition i"
	"s\n"
	"still made.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Errors\n"
	"`lambda_unexpected_character`, `lambda_expected_term`,\n"
	"`lambda_expected_parameter`, `lambda_unbalanced_parenthesis`,\n"
	"`lambda_number_too_big`, `lambda_no_normal_form`, `lambda_too_big` and\n"
	"`lambda_bad_definition`.\n";

static const char text_matrices [] =
	"id: matrices\n"
	"title: Matrices and vectors\n"
	"summary: Matrix literals, products, determinants and inverses.\n"
	"keywords: matrix, vector, determinant, inverse, transpose, rank, rref,"
	" dot, cross\n"
	"see: syntax\n"
	"\n"
	"A matrix is a list of rows: `[[1,2],[3,4]]`. A list of numbers\n"
	"`[1,2,3]` is a column vector. The usual operators work on them: `A*B`,\n"
	"`A^T` for the transpose, `A^-1` for the inverse.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Functions\n"
	"`det inv transpose trace rank rref solve identity dot cross norm`.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"[[1,2],[3,4]] * [[0,1],[1,0]]\n"
	"=> [[2, 1], [4, 3]]\n"
	"det([[1,2],[3,4]])\n"
	"=> -2\n"
	"inv([[1,2],[3,4]])\n"
	"=> [[-2, 1], [3/2, -1/2]]\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"Matrices are shown typeset under their line in the editor, and as real\n"
	"matrices in the [export](export).\n";

static const char text_symbolic [] =
	"id: symbolic\n"
	"title: Symbolic math\n"
	"summary: Simplify, expand, differentiate, integrate, limits and equati"
	"ons.\n"
	"keywords: simplify, expand, subs, diff, derivative, integrate, integra"
	"l, limit, solve, equation, symbols\n"
	"see: syntax, commands\n"
	"\n"
	"Inside these functions names that have no value are symbols, so\n"
	"`diff(x^3, x)` works without defining `x`. Outside them, a name with n"
	"o\n"
	"value is an error unless symbols are turned on with `:symbols on` (see\n"
	"[commands](commands)); the examples on this page are written with symb"
	"ols\n"
	"on.\n"
	"\n"
	"# The functions\n"
	"- `simplify(f)` and `expand(f)` rewrite an expression.\n"
	"- `subs(f, x, v)` puts a value for a name.\n"
	"- `diff(f, x [, n])` the derivative, `n` times.\n"
	"- `integrate(f, x [, a, b])` an antiderivative, or the definite integr"
	"al\n"
	"  from `a` to `b`.\n"
	"- `limit(f, x, a [, side])` a limit; `inf` is infinity.\n"
	"- `solve(f, x)` the real roots of `f` as a set.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"simplify(x + x)\n"
	"=> 2x\n"
	"expand((x+1)^2)\n"
	"=> x\302\262 + 2x + 1\n"
	"diff(x^3, x)\n"
	"=> 3x\302\262\n"
	"integrate(x^2, x)\n"
	"=> x\302\263/3\n"
	"integrate(x^2, x, 0, 1)\n"
	"=> 1/3\n"
	"limit(sin(x)/x, x, 0)\n"
	"=> 1\n"
	"solve(x^2 - 4, x)\n"
	"=> {-2, 2}\n"
	"```\n";

static const char text_syntax [] =
	"id: syntax\n"
	"title: The algebra syntax\n"
	"summary: Numbers, operators, names and statements of a math line.\n"
	"keywords: precedence, operators, implicit multiplication, fractions, e"
	"xact, unicode, assignment\n"
	"see: functions, units, symbolic\n"
	"\n"
	"A math line in the algebra domain is an expression, an assignment\n"
	"(`a = 3`) or the definition of a function (`f(x) = x^2`). Results are "
	"exact\n"
	"whenever they can be: fractions stay fractions.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Numbers and operators\n"
	"Numbers are written `12`, `3.14` or `.5`. The operators are `+ - * / ^"
	"`\n"
	"and the postfix `!` for the factorial. `^` binds tighter than `*` and "
	"`/`,\n"
	"and is right associative; a minus in front binds looser than `^`.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"1/3 + 1/6\n"
	"=> 1/2\n"
	"-2^2\n"
	"=> -4\n"
	"2^3^2\n"
	"=> 512\n"
	"5!\n"
	"=> 120\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Implicit products\n"
	"A number, a closing parenthesis or a superscript directly followed by "
	"a\n"
	"name, an opening parenthesis or a root is a product: `2x`, `2(x+1)`,\n"
	"`(a)(b)`, `2pi`. Names are whole words, so `xy` is one name, not `x*y`"
	".\n"
	"\n"
	"# Unicode\n"
	"The unicode spellings are the same as the ASCII ones: `x\302\262` for "
	"`x^2`,\n"
	"`\342\210\232` for `sqrt`, `\317\200` for `pi`, `\303\227` and `"
	"\302\267` for `*`, `\303\267` for `/`, `\342\210\222` for `-`.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Names and statements\n"
	"Names are letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit. A name\n"
	"that is assigned keeps its value in the lines below.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"a = 3\n"
	"=> 3\n"
	"2a + 1\n"
	"=> 7\n"
	"f(x) = x^2 + 1\n"
	"f(a)\n"
	"=> 10\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Exact and approximate results\n"
	"A result that cannot be an exact fraction is approximate and shown wit"
	"h\n"
	"`\342\211\210`: `sqrt(2)` is `\342\211\210 1.41421356`. The constants "
	"`pi` and `e` are exact\n"
	"names that evaluate to approximations.\n";

static const char text_themes [] =
	"id: themes\n"
	"title: Themes and settings\n"
	"summary: Colours, the built in themes, your own theme files and the se"
	"ttings file.\n"
	"keywords: theme, colors, colours, dark, light, contrast, mono, setting"
	"s, settings.mlcfg, background, highlight, no_color\n"
	"see: commands\n"
	"\n"
	"Math lines are drawn with syntax highlighting: numbers, names, functio"
	"ns,\n"
	"constants, units, operators, brackets, keywords and errors each have a\n"
	"colour, and so have the gutter, the results and the hints.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Choosing a theme\n"
	"`:theme dark`, `:theme light`, `:theme contrast` or `:theme mono`; Tab\n"
	"completes the names, also those of your own theme files. `:colors 24bi"
	"t`,\n"
	"`256`, `16` or `off` sets how many colours the terminal is asked to sh"
	"ow;\n"
	"it is found from `COLORTERM`, `TERM` and `NO_COLOR` by default. The ch"
	"oice is\n"
	"kept in the settings file.\n"
	"\n"
	"The `light` and `contrast` themes paint their own background; `dark` a"
	"nd\n"
	"`mono` keep the colours of the terminal.\n"
	"\n"
	"# Your own theme\n"
	"A theme is a text file `name.theme` in the settings folder. Each line "
	"is\n"
	"`class = style`:\n"
	"\n"
	"```code\n"
	"background = #ffffff\n"
	"paint      = on\n"
	"number     = #098658\n"
	"error      = #cd3131 underline\n"
	"gutter     = #6e7781\n"
	"```\n"
	"A style is a colour `#rrggbb`, a background `on #rrggbb` and the words\n"
	"`bold`, `dim`, `underline`, `reverse`; `none` is no style. Classes tha"
	"t are\n"
	"left out keep the style of the dark theme. `matlog theme dark` prints "
	"a\n"
	"built in theme in this form to start from; `:theme reload` reads the f"
	"ile\n"
	"again, and it is read again by itself when you save it.\n"
	"\n"
	"The classes are text, number, name, function, constant, unit, operator"
	",\n"
	"paren, keyword, logic, binder, error, match, unmatched, gutter, result"
	", result-error,\n"
	"ghost, hint-key, note and note-code.\n"
	"\n"
	"# The settings file\n"
	"`settings.mlcfg` in the settings folder (`%APPDATA%\\matlog` on Window"
	"s,\n"
	"`~/.config/matlog` on Linux; `MATLOG_CONFIG` names another folder). It"
	" is\n"
	"a plain text file with `[sections]` and `key = value` lines; matlog ke"
	"eps\n"
	"what it does not know when it writes. `[display]` holds `theme` and\n"
	"`colors`; `[folds]` holds the folded blocks of each session file.\n";

static const char text_units [] =
	"id: units\n"
	"title: Units\n"
	"summary: Quantities with units, conversions, SI prefixes and plural na"
	"mes.\n"
	"keywords: unit, quantity, conversion, to, meter, second, hour, day, we"
	"ek, month, year, ampere hour, prefix\n"
	"see: syntax\n"
	"\n"
	"A number followed by a unit is a quantity. Quantities add and multiply\n"
	"with their dimensions checked, and `a to b` converts.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"5 km + 300 m\n"
	"=> 5300 m\n"
	"3 ft to m\n"
	"=> 1143/1250 m\n"
	"2 hours to min\n"
	"=> 120 min\n"
	"```\n"
	"\n"
	"# Names\n"
	"Units have a symbol (`km`) and a long name (`kilometer`); the SI prefi"
	"xes\n"
	"work on both, and a long name may be plural (`3 days`). Other time uni"
	"ts are\n"
	"the `week`, the `month` (a twelfth of a year) and the `year` of 365 da"
	"ys.\n"
	"Charge can be written in ampere hours: `5 mAh to C` is `18 C`.\n"
	"\n"
	"A unit name that is also a variable means the variable: names are look"
	"ed up\n"
	"as variables first.\n"
	"\n"
	"`dim(x)` gives the dimension of a quantity as one SI unit.\n"
	"\n"
	"```example\n"
	"1 year to days\n"
	"=> 365 days\n"
	"5 mAh to C\n"
	"=> 18 C\n"
	"```\n";

